y hypothesis  pioneered by roger sperry  for how developing neurons find their  appropriate partners  during development during all this work  joshua  josh also became a major technical  innovator in neuroscience  introducing many of the molecular and  cellular methods used by neuroscientists  around the world  including many of the people on the zoom  call including the  recombinant retroviruses for tracing  cell lineage  and fluorescent transgenic mice for  visualizing neural connections including  the now famous  rainbow mouse finally in the most recent  phase of his career  josh has become one of the most foremost  anatomists of the retina  using single cell transcomics to  identify  many previously unknown cell types and  in a series of really elegant studies  has started to bridge the gaps  between gene expression anatomy neuronal  function  and even behavior  so we much very much look forward to  hearing josh tell us about his work  directly josh well thank you very much  um i'm really honored uh as you might  imagine by the receipt of this award  um and pleased to have the opportunity  to tell you about some of our research  it's especially meaningful in view of ed  skolnik's many contributions  and those of the eminences who preceded  me  before i start i also want to note how  much i admire the neuroscience community  at mit generally and at the mcgovern  institute in particular as we worked at  harvard to build cbs and hbi  um you guys have been an inspiration and  although i shouldn't say it  a source of occasional envy  now bob suggested that i include some  background  before telling you about recent projects  that's why i  asked him to minimize that part and i  decided to put together  a micro scientific autobiography as  prelude  to what you might rather hear and as i  did that i realized that my scientific  journey i hadn't quite appreciated this  before had a  strong ascending phylogenetic component  my first laboratory experience in fact  was um in late high school and early  college  in the laboratory of a wonderful man  named robert guthrie  in buffalo where i grew up and he had  developed bacterial assays for  detecting inborn errors of metabolism in  particular  phenylketonuria he was  ahead of his time in promoting science  education for students  and regularly invited students into his  laboratory  and gave us the quite unusual  opportunity to actually be useful  screening bacillus subtlest mutants that  could be used in new tests  then towards the end of college i had  the even more extraordinary opportunity  to work on protein kinases  in the laboratory of paul greengard who  was not only one of the  greatest molecular neurobiologists of  the last century  but also a brilliant and loving man who  remained a friend and mentor  to me until his death just under two  years ago  now i went to paul greengard's  laboratory because my interest in  neurobiology began  with the desire to learn something about  the biological basis of mental illness  which was really also what motivated  paul  when i got to graduate school though it  became apparent  that available methods really weren't up  to the task and so i  turned to developmental neurobiology  with a sort of vague idea which now  turns out to be likely true that  many psychiatric diseases may be  neurodevelopmental diseases  particularly diseases in which something  goes wrong with synaptic development  so for my phd thesis i climbed up  to uh manduka sexta working  on sensory neurons and their development  as the  manduka caterpillar turns into the  beautiful  manduka moth and my advisor there was  john hildebrand who i  always thank for being especially kind  and understanding of me at a time when i  was far  more obnoxious and high strung than i am  today  now during thesis um i took advantage of  the opportunity to get closer to  synaptic development by arranging a  postdoctoral fellowship  that began with work on the  neuromuscular junction of the f